  "final_answer_preview": "According to Section 353(1) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, making, publishing, or circulating any false statement or information can constitute an offence. If the statement is made with the intent to harm someone's reputation, it may be classified as defamation. However, if the person spreading the statement has reasonable grounds to believe it is true and acts in good faith, it may not amount to an offence as per Section 353(3) Exception. Additionally, Section 356(1) Explanation 3 indicates that certain forms of imputation, even if expressed ironically, can also be considered defamation. Therefore, if the false statement was made without reasonable belief in its truth and with intent to harm, it could lead to legal consequences.",
